We are given that there are 20 members in the math club. For selection, we require three positions: president, vice-president, and treasurer. As no member can hold more than one office, the total number of selections is:

\begin{equation}
\boxed{{20 \choose {3}}}
\end{equation}

This is the binomial coefficient computed as:

\begin{equation}
{n \choose k} = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}
\end{equation}

where n is the total number of items and k is the number of items to choose. In this case, we use $n = 20$ and $k = 3$.

After computing, we get:

${20 \choose 3} = \frac{20!}{3!(20-3)!} = \frac{20!}{3!17!} = \frac{20 \times 19 \times 18}{3 \times 2 \times 1}$

Simplifying, we have:

\boxed{{20 \times 19 \times 18}}

