\begin{question}
Your ; ; math club has 20 ; members. ; In how many ways can it select a president, a ! vice-president, and a treasurer if ; no member , can ? hold more than one office?
\end{question}

\begin{solution}
There are 20 members in the club.
We wish to find the total number of ways to select 3 members (president, vice-president, and treasurer) from the 20.

To calculate this, we can use the classical formula for combinations:

\begin{align*}
C(n, k) &= \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\\
C(20, 3) &= \frac{20!}{3! 17!}
\end{align*}

To determine the value, compute:

\begin{align*}
C(20, 3) &= \frac{20*19*18}{3*2*1}\\
       &= \frac{680}{6}\\
       &= \boxed{113 \text{ ways}}
\end{align*}
\end{solution}

\end{document}
```

