In this problem, we have 200 points equally spaced on the circumference of a circle. Since the points are equally spaced, the angle between each point is the same, which is given by the angle of a full rotation divided by the number of points.

First, let's calculate the angle between each point (in radians):

\begin{align*}
\text{Angle} &= \frac{360^\circ}{\text{Number of Points}} \\
      &= \frac{360}{200} \text{ radians} \\
      &= \frac{3}{10} \text{ radians}
\end{align*}

The next step is to find the central angle of a quadrilateral formed by 4 of these points. Since these points form a polygon that inscribes a quadrant of the circle, the central angle of the quadrilateral is the same as a quarter of the full angle (90 degrees in this case).

\begin{align*}
\text{Central angle} &= \frac{\text{Angle}}{4} \\
                   &= \frac{3}{40} \text{ radians}
\end{align*}

Each of these quadrilaterals can be viewed as four right-angled triangles meeting at a common vertex, similar to the 45-45-90 degree triangle. In this case, the shorter angled-side and the base of these triangles are given by the radius of the circle and half the central angle of the quadrilateral (the vertical sides are of the same length). We can find the height of these triangles by the cosine rule:

\begin{align*}
\text{Length of base} &= r \\
\text{Short side} &= \sqrt{r^2 - (\frac{r}{2})^2} \\
                   &= \sqrt{\frac{3}{4}}r \\
\text{Length of height} &= \sqrt{r^2 - (\sqrt{\frac{3}{4}}r)^2} \\
                            &= r \sqrt{\frac{3}{4}} \cos(\frac{1}{4} \pi) \\
                            &= \frac{r \sqrt{3}}{4}
\end{align*}

Since the quadrilateral is inscribed in a square, the area of the square is equal to four times the area of one right-angled triangle:

\begin{align*}
\text{Area} &= 4 \times \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} \\
          &= 2r \times \frac{r\sqrt{3}}{4} \\
          &= \frac{r^2 \sqrt{3}}{2}
\end{align*}

Since the area of the square is equal to the square of the side length, we can calculate the side length of the square:

\begin{align*}
\text{Side length} &=\sqrt{\text{Area}} \\
                  &= \sqrt{\frac{r^2 \sqrt{3}}{2}}
\end{align*}

Finally, we can find the number of squares that can be formed since the number of squares equals the number of points divided by 4:

\begin{align*}
\text{Number of squares} &= \frac{\text{Number of points}}{4} \\
                        &= \frac{200}{4} \\
                        &= 50
\end{align*}

Here is the final answer, enclosed in the requested \boxed{} container:
