To solve this problem, we need to find a projection matrix that when multiplied with an arbitrary vector in $\mathbb{R}^3$, results in a vector that lies on the $y$-axis and has the same length as the original vector.

Since all vectors on the $y$-axis have zero $x$- and $z$-components, the projection matrix should zero out these components. However, leaving the $y$-component unchanged ensures that the length of the projection is the same as the original vector.

The general format for a matrix that does this for an arbitrary vector $(x, y, z)$ is:

$$
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
z
\end{pmatrix}
$$

The resulting vector will have zero $x$- and $z$-components, effectively projecting onto the $y$-axis. To obtain the exact matrix, we don't need to compute each element precisely since it's obvious they should all be zero except for the middle element in the middle row, which should be 1. So, the projection matrix onto the $y$-axis is:

$$
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & \framebox{1} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
}
$$
