The binomial coefficient $\binom{n}{r}$ can be computed using the following formula:

$$\binom{n}{r} = \dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}$$

Where $n! = n \cdot (n-1) \cdot (n-2) \ldots 2 \cdot 1$.

Recall that $0! = 1$. We have $\binom{14}{7} = 3432$ and $\binom{14}{8} = 3003$. We want to find $\binom{15}{7}$.

First, let's compute $(15-7)!$, $(15-8)!$, and $(14)!$:

\begin{align*}
 15! &= 15 \cdot 14! \\
 8! &= 8 \cdot 7 \cdot 6 \cdot 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1 \\
 7! &= 7 \cdot 6 \cdot 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1
\end{align*}

Next, compute $\frac{(15-7)!}{(14-7)!}$ using the formulas we derived earlier:

\begin{align*}
\binom{15}{7} &= \dfrac{15!}{(15-7)! \cdot (7!)^2}  % lots of space before and after the equal sign!
\\[3ex]
&= \dfrac{15!}{8! \cdot (7!)^2}  % cancel 14! with $(14)!$
\\[3ex]
% next we'll cancel out common factors $c \cdot 7!$ where $c > 1$
&= \dfrac{15 \cdot 14}{2 \cdot 2} \cdot \dfrac{13 \cdot 12}{2 \cdot 2} \cdot \dfrac{11 \cdot 10}{2 \cdot 2} \cdot \dfrac{9 \cdot 8}{2 \cdot 2} \cdot \dfrac{7}{1} \cdot \dfrac{1}{7} \cdot \dfrac{1}{7}  % expand!
\\[3ex]
&= \left(\dfrac{15 \cdot 14}{2^2} \cdot \dfrac{13 \cdot 12}{2^2} \cdot \dfrac{11 \cdot 10}{2^2} \cdot \dfrac{9 \cdot 8}{2^2} \right) \cdot \dfrac{1}{7^2}  % cancel out remaining factors $7!$
\end{align*}

Now let's examine the game theory problem we have constructed. We are given some values for $\binom{n}{r}$, and we need to find a new binomial coefficient $\binom{m}{r'}$ for some other values of $m, r'$. We can construct a new set of values for possible $\binom{n}{r}$ for smaller values of $n \leq m$ based on our new values and the identity $\binom{n}{r} = \binom{n-1}{r} + \binom{n-1}{r-1}$.

