In LaTeX, I would present the solution as follows:

A function is even if and only if $f(-x) = f(x)$. A function is odd if and only if $f(-x) = -f(x)$. Now suppose a function $f(x)$ is both even and odd. By the definition above, we have

\begin{align*}
f(-x) &= f(x) \\[-.3ex]
&= -f(x)
\end{align*}
Since we consider real numbers as the domain, these two expressions must be equal when $x$ is any real number. Hence we can deduce that $f(x) = 0$ for all $x$, because only such a function is both even and odd on the entire domain of real numbers. Therefore, there is only one such function.

\boxed{ 0 }

