The volume of a cube can be expressed as \boxed{\text{length}^3} if a side has length `l`.
With the provided volume of \boxed{0.027 cubic meters}, first we need to find the volume in cubic meters (m^3) because the expression for the surface area requires a cube length (and not a cube side). The surface area is expressed as \boxed{6 \text{ times } \text{ the square of the length}}.

Now, we convert the given volume of 0.027 cubic meters to cubic meters only (not the cubes, but just the number itself is m^3). Since the side length 'l' of the cube is still unknown, we'll reference it as 'l'.  The cube side length 'l' will be obtained while finding the cube root of the specified volume.

\begin{align*}
0.027 & = l^3 \\
\sqrt[3]{0.027} &= l
\end{align*}

Calculate the cube root of 0.027:
\begin{align*}
l &= \sqrt[3]{0.027} \\
&= 0.3465
\end{align*}

Finally, calculate the surface area:
\begin{align*}
\text{surface area} &= 6 \text{ times } \text{ the square of the length} \\
&= 6 \left( 0.3465 \right) ^2 \\
&= 6 \left( 0.1219 \right) \\
&= \boxed{0.7318} \text{ square meters }
\end{align*}

